[
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-01",
    "question": "<p>The complex numbers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{1}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{2}\"></span> are given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{1}=2-i\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{2}=-8+9 i\"></span></p><p>(a) Show <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{1}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{2}\"></span> on a single Argand diagram. <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p><p>Find, showing your working,</p><p>(b) the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left| z_{1} \\right|\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(c) the value of arg <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{1}\"></span> , giving your answer in radians to 2 decimal places, <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(d) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{z_{2}}{z_{1}}\"></span> in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a+bi\"></span> , where a and b are real. <span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Think of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2-i\"></span> as the point reached by moving two units right and one down. Its distance from the origin is a hypotenuse and its argument is a clockwise turn. The reference triangle has horizontal side <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\"></span> and vertical side <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1\"></span>, so its acute angle has tangent <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/2\"></span>. For the division, choose the conjugate because <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2-i)(2+i)=2^2+1^2\"></span> is real. As a check, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-5+2i)(2-i)=-8+9i\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Plot the real part horizontally and the imaginary part vertically. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<figure class=\"old-spec-diagram\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-01-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked diagram for REV01-01\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;max-width:100%;max-height:65mm\"></figure>",
      "<p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|z_1|=\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) The point is in the fourth quadrant, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\arg z_1=-\\arctan(1/2)=-0.46\"></span> radians (2 d.p.). <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(d) Multiply numerator and denominator by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2+i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac{(-8+9i)(2+i)}{(2-i)(2+i)}=\\dfrac{-16-8i+18i-9}{4+1}=-5+2i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>A second way to do (d) is to put the quotient equal to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a+bi\"></span>. Then</p>",
      "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"(2-i)(a+bi)=(2a+b)+(2b-a)i=-8+9i.\"></div>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2a+b=-8\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2b-a=9\"></span>. From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b=-8-2a\"></span> the second equation becomes <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-16-5a=9\"></span>, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=-5\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b=2\"></span>.</p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 8 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-01-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2009 FP1 · Q1, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-02",
    "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(x)=(x^{2}+4)(x^{2}+8x+25)\"></div><p>(a) Find the four roots of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(x)=0.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(b) Find the sum of these four roots.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The zero-product rule lets us solve two quadratics independently. In the first, the distance from zero along the imaginary axis is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\"></span>. In the second, completing the square moves the centre to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-4\"></span> on the real axis. Each pair then contributes twice its real part to the sum.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Set each factor equal to zero. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2=-4\"></span> obtain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\pm2i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>For the second factor, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2+8x+25=(x+4)^2+9\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x+4)^2=-9\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=-4\\pm3i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Add the two conjugate pairs. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The imaginary terms cancel, leaving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-8\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-02-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2009 FP1 · Q3, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-03",
    "question": "<p>Solve the equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^{3}=4\\sqrt{2}-4\\sqrt{2} \\mathrm{i},\"></span> giving your answers in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r(\\cos \\theta+\\mathrm{i}\\sin \\theta),\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\pi&lt;\\theta\\leq\\pi.\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Cubing triples the angle, so angles differing by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\pi\"></span> before division by three lead to distinct answers afterwards. For example, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-\\pi/4-2\\pi)/3=-3\\pi/4\"></span>. Adding a fourth choice would repeat the first point after a full turn.</p>",
      "<p>The modulus of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4\\sqrt2-4\\sqrt2\\,i\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt{32+32}=8\"></span>, and its principal argument is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\pi/4\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Write <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)\"></span>. By de Moivre, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^3=8\"></span>, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r=2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The arguments obey <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3\\theta=-\\pi/4+2k\\pi\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Adjacent roots differ in argument by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\pi/3\"></span>.\nTaking <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=-1,0,1\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta=-3\\pi/4,-\\pi/12,7\\pi/12\"></span>, all in the required interval. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The three answers are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)\"></span> for these three values. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 6 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-03-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2009 FP2 · Q2, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-04",
    "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"z=2-3 \\mathrm{i}\"></div><p>(a) Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^{2}=-5-12 \\mathrm{i}.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>Find, showing your working,</p><p>(b) the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left| z^{2} \\right|,\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(c) the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\operatorname{arg} \\left(z^{2}\\right),\"></span> giving your answer in radians to 2 decimal places.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(d) Show z and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^{2}\"></span> on a single Argand diagram.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The mixed term in the square is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2(2)(-3i)\"></span>. Once both coordinates are negative, a positive calculator value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\arctan(12/5)\"></span> is only the acute angle to the horizontal. Subtracting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi\"></span> measures the clockwise turn from the positive real axis.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2-3i)^2=4-12i+9i^2=-5-12i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|z^2|=\\sqrt{(-5)^2+(-12)^2}=\\sqrt{169}=13\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) The point lies below the negative real axis. Its principal argument is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\pi+\\arctan(12/5)=-1.97\"></span> radians (2 d.p.). <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(d) Plot the two labelled points. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<figure class=\"old-spec-diagram\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-04-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked diagram for REV01-04\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;max-width:100%;max-height:65mm\"></figure>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-04-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2010 FP1 · Q1, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-05",
    "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(x)=x^{3}+x^{2}+44 x+150\"></div><p>Given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(x)=(x+3)(x^{2}+a x+b)\"></span> , where a and b are real constants,</p><p>(a) find the value of a and the value of b.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Find the three roots of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(x)=0.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p>(c) Find the sum of the three roots of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(x)=0.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The leading factor already supplies one root. To find the other two, notice that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2-2x+50=(x-1)^2+49\"></span>. The extra <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"49\"></span> explains both the imaginary displacement <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"7i\"></span> and why both roots have real part <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Expanding gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^3+(a+3)x^2+(b+3a)x+3b\"></span>. Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a+3=1\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3b=150\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=-2,\\ b=50\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The linear factor gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=-3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The quadratic satisfies <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x-1)^2+49=0\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1+7i\"></span> or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1-7i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-3+(1+7i)+(1-7i)=-1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-05-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2010 FP1 · Q4, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-06",
    "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"z=-8+(8\\sqrt{3})\\mathrm{i}\"></div><p>(a) Find the modulus of z and the argument of z.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p><p>Using de Moivre&#x27;s theorem,</p><p>(b) find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^{3}\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(c) find the values of w such that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"w^{4}=z\"></span> , giving your answers in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a+\\mathrm{i} b\"></span> , where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a,b\\in\\mathbb{R}.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>A number in the second quadrant with reference angle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi/3\"></span> has angle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi-\\pi/3\"></span>. On taking fourth roots, divide the whole expression <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\pi/3+2k\\pi\"></span> by four. This gives a quarter-turn between neighbouring answers, which checks the signs in the Cartesian list.</p>",
      "<p>(a) The modulus is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt{64+192}=16\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The argument is in the second quadrant with reference angle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\arctan\\sqrt3=\\pi/3\"></span>, hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\arg z=2\\pi/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^3=16^3(\\cos2\\pi+i\\sin2\\pi)=4096\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) The fourth roots have modulus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"16^{1/4}=2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Their arguments are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi/6+k\\pi/2\"></span>, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=0,1,2,3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substituting these angles gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt3+i,\\ -1+i\\sqrt3,\\ -\\sqrt3-i,\\ 1-i\\sqrt3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 10 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-06-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2010 FP2 · Q4, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-07",
    "question": "<p>A complex number z is represented by the point P in the Argand diagram.</p><p>(a) Given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"| z-6|=|z|\"></span> , sketch the locus of P.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Find the complex numbers z which satisfy both <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"| z-6|=|z|\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"| z-3-4i|=5.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>To see the bisector algebraically, square the two distances. The equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2+y^2=(x-6)^2+y^2\"></span> reduces to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"12x=36\"></span>, with no restriction on <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span>. On that line the circle&#x27;s radius is vertical, so its intersections lie five units above and below <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=4\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Equal distances from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(0,0)\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(6,0)\"></span> give the perpendicular bisector <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<figure class=\"old-spec-diagram\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-07-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked diagram for REV01-07\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;max-width:100%;max-height:65mm\"></figure>",
      "<p>(b) The second condition is a circle with centre <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3,4)\"></span> and radius 5. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Set <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=3\"></span> in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x-3)^2+(y-4)^2=25\"></span> to obtain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y-4=\\pm5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=3+9i\"></span> or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=3-i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 5 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-07-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2010 FP2 · Q6, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev01-08",
    "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"z_{1}=-2+\\mathrm{i}\"></div><p>(a) Find the modulus of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{1}\"></span>.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p><p>(b) Find, in radians, the argument of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{1}\"></span> , giving your answer to 2 decimal places.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>The solutions to the quadratic equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"z^{2}-10 z+28=0\"></div><p>are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{2}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{3}\"></span> .</p><p>(c) Find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{2}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{3}\"></span> , giving your answers in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p \\pm\\mathrm{i}\\sqrt{q}\"></span> , where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\"></span> are integers.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p><p>(d) Show, on an Argand diagram, the points representing your complex numbers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{1}, z_{2}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_{3}\"></span> .\n<span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-2,1)\"></span> lies above the negative real axis, so subtract the small reference angle from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi\"></span>. For the quadratic, its discriminant is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"100-112=-12\"></span>. Dividing <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt{-12}=2i\\sqrt3\"></span> by two explains the vertical coordinates of the conjugate pair.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|z_1|=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+1^2}=\\sqrt5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\arg z_1=\\pi-\\arctan(1/2)=2.68\"></span> radians (2 d.p.). <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) Completing the square gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(z-5)^2=-3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Taking square roots gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z-5=\\pm i\\sqrt3\"></span>, hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_2,z_3=5\\pm i\\sqrt3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Alternatively the quadratic formula gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(10\\pm\\sqrt{100-112})/2\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(d) Plot <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-2,1)\"></span> and the two points <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(5,\\pm\\sqrt3)\"></span> symmetrically about the real axis. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<figure class=\"old-spec-diagram\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-08-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked diagram for REV01-08\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;max-width:100%;max-height:65mm\"></figure>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 8 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev01-08-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2011 FP1 · Q2, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
